::SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Revision feedback item 1: Which choice best applies feedback to improve a speech draft?
{
~A. Ignore the feedback and keep the draft the same#This choice is incomplete because it does not improve the speech draft in a clear, audience-focused way.
=B. Revise the draft in a way that responds directly to useful feedback#Correct. This choice improves the speech by using feedback, clarity, cohesion, or standard English to support audience understanding.
~C. Add a random fact that does not fit the point#This choice reflects a common misconception. Recheck the feedback or sentence connection before revising.
~D. Make the draft longer but not clearer#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the audience will understand the speech.
}
::SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.04
Question:
Revision feedback item 2: Which choice best applies feedback to improve a speech draft?
{
~A. Ignore the feedback and keep the draft the same#This choice is incomplete because it does not improve the speech draft in a clear, audience-focused way.
~B. Change every sentence without checking purpose#This choice may sound related, but it does not fully address revision, clarity, cohesion, or standard speech communication.
~C. Add a random fact that does not fit the point#This choice reflects a common misconception. Recheck the feedback or sentence connection before revising.
=D. Add explanation that helps the audience understand the speech point#Correct. This choice improves the speech by using feedback, clarity, cohesion, or standard English to support audience understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U05_L01_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Revision feedback item 3: Which choice best applies feedback to improve a speech draft?
{
=A. Use adult, peer, or online feedback with judgment and purpose#Correct. This choice improves the speech by using feedback, clarity, cohesion, or standard English to support audience understanding.
~B. Change every sentence without checking purpose#This choice may sound related, but it does not fully address revision, clarity, cohesion, or standard speech communication.
~C. Add a random fact that does not fit the point#This choice reflects a common misconception. Recheck the feedback or sentence connection before revising.
~D. Make the draft longer but not clearer#This choice weakens communication because it ignores how the audience will understand the speech.
}
